/// Network policy broker (`netpolicy`): grants flow capabilities according to `netpolicy.txt` on the boot disk
/// (MC-11.6). A grant is an endpoint capability of the network stack with its own badge; the stack lets its holder
/// reach only the destinations, for the term and up to the volume the policy names for the program. Every grant,
/// refusal and revocation is written to the system log. Its clients are launchers (the shell).
///
/// 1.1 (108): the policy can be changed while the system runs. The changed policy is kept in the broker's private
/// directory of the boot disk (`system/netpolicy/netpolicy.txt`, which only the broker opens), and the broker reads it
/// in place of the shipped `netpolicy.txt`. Only a launcher holds a client: the shell asks the user before it adds or
/// removes a line. Grants made before a change keep their rules; the next grant follows the change.
///
/// 1.2 (800-NET-0038): a client badged `mind::network::BADGE_LAUNCH` may only launch: it prepares, takes and binds
/// grants and reads the policy and the grants, and is refused `revoke`, `add` and `remove` (`denied`), as is a client
/// with any other badge. The shell's client has no badge; `link` gets a launcher's in its developer mode, as it has no
/// user to ask.
///
/// 1.3 (000-NET-0049): a running program asks, through its grant (`socket.wit` 2.8), for a TCP flow its grant does not
/// name. The broker names the program and its PID from its grants and gives the question to the shell (`questions`),
/// which puts it to the user on the program's console and gives the answer (`answer`). A question no one answers within
/// 60 s is refused, and one whose program ended leaves the list. A launcher's client is refused both (`denied`).
package mind:netpolicy@1.3.0;

interface netpolicy {
    /// `unwritable` (1.1): the change could not be stored, so it was not made. `denied` (1.2): a launcher's client asked
    /// to change the policy or to revoke a grant.
    enum error { no-policy, no-network, limit, not-found, invalid, unwritable, denied }

    /// A live grant: its badge, the program it was made for, rules, milliseconds left, bytes used.
    record grant { badge: u16, program: string<16>, rules: u8, left-ms: u32, used: u64 }

    /// Makes a grant for `program` from the policy; returns its badge. Error no-policy: the policy names nothing for it.
    prepare: func(program: string<16>) -> result<u16, error>;
    /// The grant with `badge` as a stack client to lend to that program (a word call, so it can carry the capability).
    take: func(badge: u16) -> result<borrow<endpoint>, error>;
    /// Ties grant `badge` to the process the launcher started with it: the grant ends when that process does.
    bind: func(badge: u16, pid: u64) -> result<_, error>;
    /// Revokes every grant of `program` (its copies are removed, its flows closed); returns how many.
    revoke: func(program: string<16>) -> result<u32, error>;
    /// The live grants.
    list: func() -> list<grant, 32>;
    /// The lines of the policy in force, from line `start` (comments and blank lines left out) (1.1).
    lines: func(start: u32) -> list<string<160>, 16>;
    /// Adds `line` at the end of the policy; a line that is not a rule, a `dns` line or a `resolver` line is refused
    /// (invalid) (1.1).
    add: func(line: string<160>) -> result<_, error>;
    /// Removes every line equal to `line` (spaces aside); returns how many (1.1).
    remove: func(line: string<160>) -> result<u32, error>;

    /// A program's question (1.3): its id, the program's PID and the name its grant was made for, the host and the
    /// TCP port it asks for, and its reason in its own words.
    record question { id: u32, pid: u64, program: string<16>, host: string<253>, port: u16, why: string<80> }
    /// The user's answer (1.3): no; once (one connection); for the rest of the program's run; always (a line of the
    /// policy as well).
    enum choice { no, once, session, always }
    /// The questions that wait, oldest first, at most 8 (1.3).
    questions: func() -> result<list<question, 8>, error>;
    /// The user's `choice` for question `id` (1.3). `not-found`: it has gone (answered, refused after 60 s, or its
    /// program ended); `no-network`: the host could not be resolved, so the question was refused; `limit`: the grant
    /// holds 16 rules; `unwritable` or `invalid` with `always`: the flow is allowed for the run, the line not stored.
    answer: func(id: u32, choice: choice) -> result<_, error>;
}
